window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-4304bbddc064698e"]={"key":"ny_main-4304bbddc064698e","group":"ny_main","uid":"4304bbddc064698e","rank":40,"title":"gallowspring everstead insider diversion decision package","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are a First-Line Supervisor of Police and Detectives at Gallowspring Networks. By Friday, 13 June 2025, Karin Sheridan needs a decision package for Everstead Group’s report of insider-enabled equipment diversions in England, assessed as of 12 June 2025. Use the attached `everstead_case_notes.docx` as the factual source; it contains the relevant emails, messages, dispatch information, access records, and interview notes. Chidi Trevelyan has already told Everstead that suspected staff identities will be shared quickly, but that promise may not sit comfortably with fair, proportionate, and lawful professional judgment. Prepare `everstead_decision_memorandum.pdf`, `everstead_options_assumptions.xlsx`, and `everstead_stakeholder_briefing.pptx` as one mutually consistent package for Karin and cross-functional internal stakeholders. Compare the two defensible readings of the situation—rapid disruption through targeted interviews and a staged, intelligence-led investigation—then choose one explicitly. Project the likely outcomes, tradeoffs, timing, evidence position, and operational risks under clearly stated assumptions, and authorize the selected next action with a named owner, a practical time boundary, and a clear account of what operating position has changed. The package must actively prevent the common misuse of treating an uncorroborated interview allegation as proof or as sufficient reason to disclose an identity or take adverse action. Address how the existing promise to Everstead is to be handled without inventing authority, identities, interview content, legal powers, or financial figures. The 18-unit incident total, 11 reconciled units, 7 unaccounted units, and £31,800 replacement exposure in the case notes are fixed facts; do not create additional case figures and label any projection as an assumption or scenario. Keep the PDF to no more than three pages and the briefing deck to no more than six slides; make both usable by someone who has not followed the investigation. Put all three files in the top level of the working directory where you start, using exactly the filenames given above.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/everstead_case_notes.docx` — The sole factual source for the decision package: stakeholder communications, incident facts, dispatch and access observations, interview material, timing, and the existing client promise.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `everstead_decision_memorandum.pdf` — A concise decision memorandum that compares both defensible readings, makes one explicit recommendation, projects consequences, addresses the client promise and evidential safeguards, and authorizes the selected next action. (Maximum three pages.; Written for Karin Sheridan and cross-functional internal stakeholders.; Must identify assumptions and distinguish intelligence, allegation, and evidence.; Must end with the decision rather than an uncommitted list of possible next steps.)\n- `everstead_options_assumptions.xlsx` — An auditable workbook comparing the two options, recording factual inputs and assumptions, projecting outcomes and risks, and showing how the selected option supports the recommendation. (Use the case notes as the factual basis.; Separate fixed facts from assumptions, estimates, and scenario outputs.; Calculations must be traceable and agree with the memorandum and briefing deck.; Do not add unsupported case figures or identities.)\n- `everstead_stakeholder_briefing.pptx` — A short stakeholder briefing that communicates the situation, option comparison, chosen decision, projected implications, safeguards, and authorized action. (Maximum six slides.; Suitable for a cross-functional internal audience.; Use the same recommendation, figures, terminology, assumptions, and action ownership as the other deliverables.; Make the warning against treating an uncorroborated allegation as proof operationally clear.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/everstead_case_notes.docx","bytes":38697,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/61f4658b68f2c7c6e8e5dfa8a7c71596b191305cb59bf5b99c9cfe4e67f267a1.docx","sha256":"61f4658b68f2c7c6e8e5dfa8a7c71596b191305cb59bf5b99c9cfe4e67f267a1","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Everstead Group — case notes\nPrepared for internal incident review  |  factual position current to 12 June 2025\nIncident summary\nEverstead Group reported a possible equipment diversion on 9 June 2025. The report covers 18 equipment units across three dispatch lots dated between 22 May and 4 June 2025. Eleven units have been reconciled to authorised destinations; seven remain unaccounted for. Everstead records replacement exposure of £31,800. The decision package is required by Friday, 13 June 2025.\nDispatch lot\nWindow\nLedger observation\nReconciliation\nLot 1\n22 May 2025\nException code: CB-EXC\nauthorised destinations partly confirmed\nLot 2\n29 May 2025\nException code: CB-EXC\nauthorised destinations partly confirmed\nLot 3\n4 Jun 2025\nException code: CB-EXC\nauthorised destinations partly confirmed\nThe last known diversion-related dispatch event is 4 June 2025. A collection-bay access permission remains active through a scheduled collection on 16 June 2025. This is a continuing operational concern; it does not establish that any person will misuse it.\nRecorded material\nDispatch ledger. The same exception code appears across the three lots. The ledger does not identify who handled, redirected or received any equipment.\nAccess log. Three staff badge events overlap relevant collection windows. The record shows access events, not handling or redirection of equipment.\nInterview note. A witness reports that Employee A said Employee B arranged redirection to an unapproved collection point. The witness did not see the event and cannot independently verify the account. The note is a second-hand account.\nCurrent record position. The available records do not establish a named perpetrator, a completed criminal offence by any particular person, or a basis for immediate identity disclosure.\nStakeholder communications\nEmail — Everstead Group to Chidi Trevelyan, 10 June, 08:41\n“Please send the suspected staff names quickly. We need to decide what to tell our operations lead.”\nInternal Teams message — Chidi Trevelyan to Karin Sheridan, 10 June, 09:06\n“I have told Everstead we will share suspected staff identities quickly. Please advise how we should proceed.”\nNo record supplied here gives a disclosure authority, interview content beyond the note above, a legal power, or a client-approved identity list.\nHandling note\nThese notes distinguish recorded observations and stakeholder positions from conclusions. They do not determine a preferred investigative course or a legal outcome.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/everstead_decision_memorandum.pdf","bytes":9897,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/be42021384b888414dedd9c9d541809156d6f5be1780178b101d6cbd4eb5f3f3.pdf","sha256":"be42021384b888414dedd9c9d541809156d6f5be1780178b101d6cbd4eb5f3f3","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":" DECISION MEMORANDUM\n\n\n Everstead equipment diversion — operating decision\n To: Karin Sheridan and cross-functional internal stakeholders | Decision required: Friday, 13 June 2025 | Factual position: 12 June 2025\n\n\n  DECISION — AUTHORISE STAGED, INTELLIGENCE-LED INVESTIGATION\n  Start immediate preservation and proportionate operational controls now. Do not move to targeted\n  interviews, identity disclosure, disciplinary action, or a guilt conclusion on the interview allegation and\n  access overlap alone. Karin Sheridan owns the 13 June checkpoint.\n\n\n What is established / what is not\n Supplied record                                                            Decision significance\n\n 18 units in three dispatch lots; 11 reconciled; 7 unaccounted.             Fixed baseline: 18 − 11 = 7. It establishes impact, not responsibility.\n Client-recorded replacement exposure: £31,800.\n\n Same CB-EXC ledger exception across the lots; three badge events           Actionable intelligence / records to test. Neither record identifies\n overlap relevant collection windows.                                       handling or redirection.\n\n Witness reports that Employee A said Employee B arranged a                 Uncorroborated second-hand allegation — a lead only. It is not\n redirection. Witness did not see or independently verify it.               evidence of guilt or a finding.\n\n Access permission remains active through scheduled collection on 16        Plausible continuing operational concern; control proportionately while\n June; last known event was 4 June.                                         facts are secured.\n\n Assessment and recommendation\n Both readings are defensible. Rapid targeted interviews could disrupt a live concern and generate accounts quickly; they also\n risk alerting people, contaminating recollection, unfairness, and pressure to disclose a name before the records are tested. A\n staged approach is slower to a person-specific answer but preserves provenance, tests the common exception and access\n overlap, and contains the 16 June concern through existing, proportionate operational arrangements. Choose staged\n investigation: at this evidential stage its fairness and rework advantages outweigh the speed benefit, provided it has the firm\n 13 June review below.\n Assumptions used for the option model: the common exception and access records can be preserved and reviewed before the 13 June\n checkpoint; existing operational controls can be assessed without asserting new powers; and any interview is only considered after a\n documented review. These are professional planning assumptions, not observed case facts.\n\n\n\n\nGallowspring Networks | Internal decision package | assessed 12 June 2025                                                                         Page 1\n\f Options and projected consequences\n Dimension                    Rapid targeted interviews                                    Staged intelligence-led investigation — selected\n\n Speed / operational          Can obtain accounts quickly and may signal disruption. It    Begin preservation and operational control immediately;\n effect                       could also alert a person before ledger/access material is   reach a documented evidence review by 13 June. More\n                              secured.                                                     measured path to a person-specific step.\n\n Information / evidence       Interview accounts may create new leads, but early           Original ledger, access exports and interview note are\n position                     questioning can affect later recall and does not cure the    retained and compared for provenance, timing and\n                              second-hand allegation.                                      alternative explanations before escalation.\n\n Fairness / client risk       Higher risk of an allegation being treated as proof,         Lower disclosure and rework risk; client receives a candid\n                              premature adverse action or impermissible identity           status reset rather than an unsupported name.\n                              sharing under client pressure.\n\n Principal downside           Potentially unfair, less reliable process and                If controls are inadequate or review drifts, the active\n                              harder-to-repair client/employee harm.                       access period may leave operational exposure\n                                                                                           insufficiently managed.\n\n Scenario model               Weighted planning score: 62 / 105 (assumption-based).        Weighted planning score: 92 / 105 (assumption-based);\n                                                                                           supports selection, not a finding.\n\n\n Required safeguards and boundaries\n • Evidence gate. No identity is disclosed to Everstead, no disciplinary/adverse action is initiated, and no guilt conclusion is\n recorded solely from the second-hand allegation or badge overlap. Before a stronger step, Karin must record independent\n verification/corroboration relevant to the proposed step and obtain HR/Legal advice where employment or disclosure\n consequences arise.\n • Record integrity. Preserve original dispatch ledger extracts for all three CB-EXC lots, relevant access records, the interview\n note and available metadata; record source, collector, date/time, access and any working copy. Restrict editing and keep a\n retrieval log.\n • Fair process. If a targeted interview is later necessary, use impartial, non-accusatory questions; give a genuine opportunity\n to respond; make a reliable contemporaneous record; and arrange appropriate HR advice/representation safeguards where\n applicable. A private corporate inquiry has no automatic police powers of search, seizure or compelled interview.\n • England information handling. Treat identities and investigative material as personal data: use a lawful, fair and\n proportionate purpose; minimise access and share on need-to-know only. UK GDPR / Data Protection Act 2018 and\n employment-process issues require competent HR/Legal advice; do not imply that the client request creates authority to\n disclose.\n\n\n\n\nGallowspring Networks | Internal decision package | assessed 12 June 2025                                                                            Page 2\n\f Authorisation and changed operating position\n Owner                       Authorised action / boundary                                               When / review\n\n Karin Sheridan              Authorises the staged posture and evidence gate. Directs custody of the    By 16:00, Fri 13 Jun. Stop/hold a targeted\n Decision owner              review record; decides at checkpoint whether any person-specific step is   interview or disclosure if the material\n                             necessary and proportionate.                                               remains only allegation plus overlap, or if\n                                                                                                        integrity/fairness cannot be assured.\n\n Records custodian           Preserve/export the specified ledger, access and interview records;        By 12:00, Fri 13 Jun. Escalate promptly to\n Role owner                  record provenance and access. Assess existing operational                  Karin if a record cannot be secured or\n                             arrangements for the 16 Jun collection — no assertion of new authority     operational control cannot be assessed.\n                             and no allegation-based sanction.\n\n Chidi Trevelyan             Reset Everstead’s expectation: provide a status and safeguarding           By 13:00, Fri 13 Jun. Log the\n Client liaison              update, but no suspected identity. State that names are not shared         communication; refer any challenge to\n                             absent a substantiated, necessary and appropriately authorised basis.      Karin and HR/Legal review.\n\n\n\n  OPERATING POSITION CHANGED\n  From: “share suspected staff identities quickly.”\n  To: “preserve and test intelligence; provide Everstead a status update only; hold identities, adverse action\n  and conclusions behind a documented corroboration, necessity and authority gate.”\n\n Decision record: This authorisation is an internal operational decision, not a finding of misconduct or criminality. It does not\n create police powers or decide any employment outcome. The staged course is proportionate to the fixed incident baseline, the\n unresolved status of seven units, the active access concern and the current evidential limits.\n\n\n\n\nGallowspring Networks | Internal decision package | assessed 12 June 2025                                                                       Page 3\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}},{"name":"package/private/gold/everstead_options_assumptions.xlsx","bytes":12256,"extension":".xlsx","url":"assets/d1cadcff34889ddf1201ee3e9faa2ec2e92691479f933cc129f6251751124d78.xlsx","sha256":"d1cadcff34889ddf1201ee3e9faa2ec2e92691479f933cc129f6251751124d78","preview":{"kind":"sheets","sheets":[{"name":"Decision model","row_count":49,"rows":[{"r":"1","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"2","cells":["","Everstead Group | options & assumptions model","","","","",""]},{"r":"3","cells":["","Internal decision support — assessed 12 June 2025 | Source: everstead_case_notes.docx","","","","",""]},{"r":"4","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"5","cells":["","Selected option (formula-driven):","","=IF(C34>C33,\"Staged intelligence-led investigation\",\"Rapid targeted interviews\")  [缓存: Staged intelligence-led investigation]","","",""]},{"r":"6","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"7","cells":["","1. SUPPLIED FACTS — extracted from case notes (not scenario assumptions)","","","","",""]},{"r":"8","cells":["","Input","Value","Status","Source wording / arithmetic","Decision use",""]},{"r":"9","cells":["","Units in scope","18","Fixed fact","18 units across three dispatch lots","Impact baseline",""]},{"r":"10","cells":["","Reconciled units","11","Fixed fact","11 reconciled to authorised destinations","Impact baseline",""]},{"r":"11","cells":["","Unaccounted units","=C9-C10  [缓存: 7]","Derived fact","18 − 11 = 7","Impact baseline",""]},{"r":"12","cells":["","Replacement exposure","31800","Fixed fact","Client recorded replacement exposure","Impact baseline",""]},{"r":"13","cells":["","Record position","No named perpetrator or completed offence established","Fixed fact","Case notes current record position","No immediate identity disclosure basis",""]},{"r":"14","cells":["","Active concern","Access permission active through scheduled collection on 16 June","Fixed fact","Last known event: 4 June","Requires timely, proportionate control",""]},{"r":"15","cells":["","Evidence status","Second-hand interview account; badge overlap; common exception code","Fixed fact","Records do not establish handling/redirection","Lead/intelligence to preserve and test",""]},{"r":"16","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"17","cells":["","2. PLANNING ASSUMPTIONS — transparent scenario inputs, not case facts","","","","",""]},{"r":"18","cells":["","Criterion","Weight (1–5)","Rapid targeted interviews rating (1–5)","Staged intelligence-led rating (1–5)","Why this assumption matters","Scale"]},{"r":"19","cells":["","Speed to initial account","3","5","3","Rapid questioning is likely quicker; staged work still starts preservation now.","1 low / 5 high"]},{"r":"20","cells":["","Evidence quality / provenance","4","2","5","Preserving and comparing original records before a person-specific step improves auditability.","1 weak / 5 strong"]},{"r":"21","cells":["","Fairness / reviewability","4","2","5","Early interview on a second-hand account carries a higher avoidable fairness risk.","1 weak / 5 strong"]},{"r":"22","cells":["","Client confidence over time","3","3","4","A candid reset may be slower but reduces risk of an unsupported disclosure.","1 weak / 5 strong"]},{"r":"23","cells":["","Operational control","4","4","4","Both can address the 16 Jun concern; staged route requires custody review now.","1 weak / 5 strong"]},{"r":"24","cells":["","Rework / escalation resilience","3","2","5","Premature action may require repair; documented gating reduces rework.","1 weak / 5 strong"]},{"r":"25","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"26","cells":["","3. OPTION COMPARISON — formulas use the assumptions above","","","","",""]},{"r":"27","cells":["","Option","Speed","Evidence / fairness","Client / disclosure","Operational effect","Principal downside"]},{"r":"28","cells":["","Rapid targeted interviews","Initial accounts quickly; may disrupt concern.","Lead may develop, but allegation not cured; recollection/alerting risk.","Pressure for names; risk of premature disclosure/adverse action.","Potential immediate disruption.","Unfair or unreliable process; rework and harm if allegation is misused."]},{"r":"29","cells":["","Staged intelligence-led investigation","Preserve now; evidence checkpoint by 13 June.","Records tested for provenance, timing and alternative explanations.","Status update rather than unsupported name.","Control assessment before 16 June collection.","Exposure if control/review drifts or records cannot be secured."]},{"r":"30","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"31","cells":["","4. AUDITABLE SCENARIO OUTPUT — selected result is connected to formula comparison","","","","",""]},{"r":"32","cells":["","Option","Weighted score","Formula / result","Decision use","Projection label",""]},{"r":"33","cells":["","Rapid targeted interviews","=SUMPRODUCT($C$19:$C$24,$D$19:$D$24)  [缓存: 62]","Assumption-based score / 105","Comparable scenario result","Projection only",""]},{"r":"34","cells":["","Staged intelligence-led investigation","=SUMPRODUCT($C$19:$C$24,$E$19:$E$24)  [缓存: 92]","Assumption-based score / 105","Comparable scenario result","Projection only",""]},{"r":"35","cells":["","Model rationale","Staged score is expected to lead because integrity, fairness and resilience are weighted more heavily than immediate speed.","Do not use model score as proof or a legal threshold.","Recommendation support only","Professional scenario",""]},{"r":"36","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"37","cells":["","5. NON-NEGOTIABLE DECISION GATES / AUTHORISATION","","","","",""]},{"r":"38","cells":["","Control / owner","Required action","Timing / stop condition","Status","",""]},{"r":"39","cells":["","Karin Sheridan — decision owner","Authorise staged posture; review preserved material and necessity for a person-specific step.","By 16:00 Fri 13 Jun. HOLD interview, disclosure, adverse action or guilt conclusion if only allegation + badge overlap, or fairness/integrity is not assured.","Authorised","",""]},{"r":"40","cells":["","Records custodian — role owner","Preserve original CB-EXC ledger, relevant access records, interview note and metadata; log source, collector, access and copies. Assess existing operational arrangements for 16 Jun.","By 12:00 Fri 13 Jun. Escalate if record/control cannot be secured.","Authorised","",""]},{"r":"41","cells":["","Chidi Trevelyan — client liaison","Reset promise: status/safeguarding update only; no suspected identity without substantiated, necessary and appropriately authorised basis.","By 13:00 Fri 13 Jun. Log contact; refer challenge to Karin and HR/Legal.","Authorised","",""]},{"r":"42","cells":["","Evidence / disclosure gate","No identity disclosure, disciplinary/adverse action or guilt conclusion based solely on second-hand allegation or access overlap. Need independently verified/corroborated material relevant to the proposed step; obtain HR/Legal advice where applicable.","Applies now and through any escalation.","Mandatory","",""]},{"r":"43","cells":["","Fairness / jurisdiction","Any later interview: impartial questions, opportunity to respond, reliable record, appropriate HR advice/representation safeguards. Private inquiry has no automatic police powers. Apply UK GDPR / DPA 2018 principles: lawful, fair, proportionate, minimised, need-to-know.","Before any person-specific step.","Mandatory","",""]},{"r":"44","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"45","cells":["","6. CHANGED OPERATING POSITION","","","","",""]},{"r":"46","cells":["","From","“Share suspected staff identities quickly.”","Prior client expectation recorded in case notes.","","",""]},{"r":"47","cells":["","To","“Preserve and test intelligence; give Everstead a status update only; hold identities, adverse action and conclusions behind corroboration, necessity and authority gate.”","Selected operating position.","","",""]},{"r":"48","cells":["","","","","","",""]},{"r":"49","cells":["","Model note: edit only the yellow planning ratings/weights to test a scenario. Fixed facts and mandatory gates are not score inputs.","","","","",""]}],"truncated":false}],"note":"值与公式提取，不还原原始排版；每表最多120行、30列。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/everstead_stakeholder_briefing.pptx","bytes":40211,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/8f89c600bdea8af38042704dcc59d7287089aff0ab295e5b6455082e7c5b2a72.pptx","sha256":"8f89c600bdea8af38042704dcc59d7287089aff0ab295e5b6455082e7c5b2a72","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\n01  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nDecision: staged, intelligence-led investigation\nDecision required Friday, 13 June 2025 | cross-functional internal briefing\nPreserve and test the records now; contain the 16 June concern proportionately; do not disclose identities or make adverse findings from an uncorroborated allegation.\n18\nunits in scope\n11\nreconciled\n7\nunaccounted\n£31,800\nclient-recorded replacement exposure\nFixed arithmetic: 18 − 11 = 7. The impact baseline does not identify a responsible person or establish an offence.\nSelected posture is a decision on safe next steps — not a finding of misconduct or criminality.\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\n02  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nEvidence status: lead, record, or finding?\nThe distinction controls what we may do next\nRECORDS / INTELLIGENCE\nSame CB-EXC exception across three lots\nThree badge events overlap collection windows\n\nUseful leads to preserve and test — not proof of handling or redirection.\nALLEGATION\nWitness says Employee A said Employee B arranged redirection. Witness did not see it and cannot verify it.\n\nSecond-hand, uncorroborated allegation — lead only.\nFINDING?\nNo named perpetrator, completed offence, or immediate disclosure basis is established in the case notes.\n\nNo finding yet.\nOperational concern: access permission remains active through the scheduled 16 June collection. Control can be considered now without converting a lead into proof.\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\n03  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nBoth readings are defensible — the trade-off is visible\nAssumption-based scenario model; not an evidential threshold\nRAPID TARGETED INTERVIEWS\nBenefits\n• Faster initial accounts; potential disruption\n\nCosts\n• May alert people before records are secured\n• Higher fairness, disclosure and recollection-contamination risk\n• Allegation remains uncorroborated\n\nScenario score: 62 / 105\nSTAGED, INTELLIGENCE-LED — SELECTED\nBenefits\n• Preserve and compare original ledger/access/interview material\n• Fairer, more reviewable route; status-only client update\n• Immediate control assessment before 16 June\n\nCost\n• Slower to a person-specific step; exposure if control/review drifts\n\nScenario score: 92 / 105\nModel assumptions weight evidence quality, fairness and resilience more heavily than immediate speed. Change the documented workbook ratings to test the result.\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\n04  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nSelected posture: fast preservation, staged escalation\nA firm checkpoint avoids indefinite deferral\nNOW\nPreserve original CB-EXC ledger, relevant access records, interview note + metadata. Log source, collector, access and working copies.\n›\nBEFORE 16 JUN\nRecords custodian assesses existing operational arrangements for scheduled collection; escalate if records or control cannot be secured.\n›\n13 JUN CHECKPOINT\nKarin reviews provenance, timing and alternative explanations. Determine whether a person-specific step is necessary and proportionate.\nStop / hold: do not interview, disclose an identity, initiate adverse action or conclude guilt if the material remains only allegation + badge overlap, or integrity/fairness is not assured.\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\n05  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nSafeguards: protect people, records and client trust\nThe earlier promise is reset — it does not dictate professional judgment\nCLIENT MESSAGE\nChidi Trevelyan sends a status and safeguarding update by 13:00 Friday. No suspected identity is shared absent a substantiated, necessary and appropriately authorised basis.\nFAIR PROCESS\nIf later interview is necessary: impartial questions; opportunity to respond; reliable record; appropriate HR advice / representation safeguards.\nINFORMATION & AUTHORITY\nNeed-to-know, minimised handling of identities/investigative material. UK GDPR / DPA 2018 principles; HR/Legal advice where applicable. No automatic police powers for a private inquiry.\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\n06  |  EVERSTEAD GROUP\nAuthorisation: execute the staged posture\nClear ownership, timing and changed operating position\nKarin Sheridan | decision owner\nAuthorises posture and evidence gate; makes checkpoint decision.\nBy 16:00 Fri 13 Jun\nRecords custodian | role owner\nPreserves records and assesses existing operational arrangements.\nBy 12:00 Fri 13 Jun\nChidi Trevelyan | client liaison\nResets client expectation; logs status-only update.\nBy 13:00 Fri 13 Jun\nCHANGED POSITION  |  From “share suspected identities quickly” to “preserve and test intelligence; status update only; identities, adverse action and conclusions behind corroboration, necessity and authority gate.”\nGallowspring Networks | Internal | assessed 12 June 2025\nDecision package","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共6页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All three required deliverables are present at the top level with the exact requested filenames and are readable in their stated formats.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Working-directory file listing; PDF, XLSX, and PPTX open/render successfully","grader_hint":"Check exact case-sensitive filenames and extensions before reviewing content. This is a packaging check, not a quality substitute."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package accurately carries through the fixed case facts: 18 units in scope, 11 reconciled, 7 unaccounted, and £31,800 replacement exposure, with the arithmetic relationship intact and no unsupported identities or case figures presented as facts.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision memorandum factual summary; Workbook input and calculation areas; Briefing deck situation or decision slides","grader_hint":"Compare each stated figure with the supplied case notes and verify 18 minus 11 equals 7. Treat invented names, amounts, counts, or events as failure of this criterion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum and briefing compare rapid targeted interviews and a staged intelligence-led investigation as genuinely defensible alternatives, addressing speed, evidential quality, fairness, client or operational risk, and likely consequences rather than caricaturing one option.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Options comparison in the PDF; Scenario comparison in the XLSX; Options or tradeoff slide in the PPTX","grader_hint":"A good comparison makes the tension legible and gives each option meaningful benefits and costs. Do not require a particular option to be selected."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package makes exactly one recommendation, states it unambiguously, and uses the same selected option in the memorandum, workbook, and briefing deck.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision statement in the PDF; Selected-option field or equivalent in the XLSX; Decision statement in the PPTX","grader_hint":"Accept either option when the choice is deliberate and supported. Do not award credit where the artifacts present a tie, alternating recommendations, or a menu for Karin to decide."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected action is operationally authorized with a responsible owner, timing tied to the case deadline, a defined scope, a review or stop condition, and a clear statement of what changes from the current operating position.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Authorization and changed-position language in the PDF; Action or decision fields in the XLSX; Action slide in the PPTX","grader_hint":"Look for an executable authorization rather than a recommendation label alone. The owner may be a permitted named stakeholder or an adequately specific role, and the timing must be concrete enough to act on."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package projects outcomes for both options under explicit assumptions, including timing, expected information or evidence position, operational effect, and principal downside; the chosen option's projection supports the recommendation.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Outcome projection in the PDF; Assumption-driven option model in the XLSX; Summary of implications in the PPTX","grader_hint":"Qualitative projections are acceptable where the source does not support precision, but estimates must be labelled as assumptions or scenarios and must not be disguised as observed facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workbook clearly separates supplied facts from professional assumptions, estimates, and scenario outputs, and exposes the assumptions that materially drive the option comparison.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workbook input, assumption, and output areas; Labels, notes, formulas, or equivalent audit trail","grader_hint":"A reviewer should be able to tell what came from the case notes and what the author chose for projection. Unsupported precision or unlabeled hard-coded conclusions should not pass."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workbook's option calculations are auditable and internally coherent: changing a documented scenario assumption changes the relevant projection, and the displayed recommendation is connected to the comparison rather than being an isolated hard-coded answer.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workbook formulas and dependency structure; Recalculated workbook outputs","grader_hint":"Inspect formulas or recalculate with a changed assumption where practical. The workbook need not be elaborate, but its model must demonstrate a real relationship between inputs, options, and outputs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package consistently distinguishes actionable intelligence, an allegation, corroborated evidence, and a finding or decision, and does not present the second-hand interview account as established fact.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-status language in all three deliverables; Treatment of the interview note and access information","grader_hint":"Assess the wording and status labels in context. The account may be used as a lead or intelligence, but must not be upgraded merely because it appears in the case notes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package actively prevents misuse of the uncorroborated allegation by stating a practical decision gate against identity disclosure, disciplinary action, or a guilt conclusion based solely on that allegation and by identifying what kind of corroboration or verification is required before stronger action.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Safeguard or evidence-threshold language in the PDF; Control or gate in the XLSX; Explicit warning and safeguard in the PPTX","grader_hint":"A mere statement that the allegation is uncorroborated is insufficient. Look for an active restriction and a proportionate route to verification, without demanding a made-up numeric threshold."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package reconciles the prior promise to Everstead with professional standards by acknowledging the commitment, refusing to let it dictate an unsupported identity disclosure, and giving Karin a credible way to reset or qualify the client expectation.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Client-communications treatment in the PDF; Risk or stakeholder treatment in the XLSX; Stakeholder message in the PPTX","grader_hint":"Look for an accountable handling of the promise, not blame directed at the sender and not silent avoidance. The proposed communication must preserve trust without overstating certainty or authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum applies England-specific professional and legal boundaries as at 12 June 2025 accurately enough for an internal decision, including proportionality, fair treatment, information handling, and the limits of any police or investigative power invoked; it does not present a corporate inquiry as automatically possessing powers it does not have.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction and standards section of the PDF; Relevant caveats or citations in the PDF and PPTX","grader_hint":"Reward accurate, appropriately scoped references to applicable England standards and escalation to competent legal or HR advice where needed. Penalize overconfident legal claims, invented authority, or unexplained use of police powers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed investigative posture protects fairness in any targeted interview or later employment action through impartial questioning, a reliable record of what was said, an opportunity to respond, and appropriate advice or representation safeguards where applicable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Interview and fairness safeguards in the PDF; Risk controls in the XLSX; Interview safeguard summary in the PPTX","grader_hint":"Do not require a single prescribed interview script. Check whether the package makes the selected course fair and reviewable rather than merely fast."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package addresses preservation and integrity of relevant dispatch, access, interview, and other records so that information can be evaluated later without avoidable alteration, loss, or uncertainty about provenance.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-management controls in the PDF; Risk/control treatment in the XLSX","grader_hint":"Look for practical preservation, provenance, access, or audit language. A generic instruction to 'collect evidence' without integrity or recordkeeping safeguards is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Identity and investigative information are handled on a necessity, proportionality, and need-to-know basis, with attention to data minimization and controlled disclosure to the client and internal stakeholders.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Information-sharing safeguards in the PDF; Disclosure risk in the XLSX; Audience-facing safeguard in the PPTX","grader_hint":"The artifact should distinguish internal decision support from disclosure of a suspected person's identity. Do not award credit for confidentiality language that leaves the sharing decision unrestricted."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation states material uncertainty and tradeoffs, identifies what is known versus unresolved, and explains why the chosen option is proportionate despite the competing defensible reading.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Reasoning and limitations in the PDF; Confidence or uncertainty treatment in the XLSX; Rationale in the PPTX","grader_hint":"A strong package does not manufacture certainty to make the decision look easy. It makes the bounded judgment visible and ties it to the supplied facts and assumptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The briefing deck contains no more than six slides and communicates the decision, rationale, projected implications, safeguards, and authorized action without requiring the audience to read dense unstructured prose.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered PPTX slide count and visual review","grader_hint":"Count rendered slides and assess whether the content is legible and usable in a stakeholder briefing. Do not reward a compliant slide count if essential content is unreadable or absent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The decision memorandum is no more than three pages and remains readable as a decision document, with a visible recommendation, supporting comparison, assumptions, safeguards, and authorization.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered PDF page count and visual review","grader_hint":"Count PDF pages after rendering. Check that the page cap has not been met by shrinking text to an unusable size or omitting the decision basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workbook, memorandum, and deck agree on option names, the selected course, the 18/11/7 unit relationship, the £31,800 exposure, key assumptions, evidence-status terminology, and action ownership or timing.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Side-by-side review of all three deliverables","grader_hint":"Treat meaningful contradictions as failure even if each file is individually polished. Minor formatting differences are acceptable; conclusions, figures, assumptions, and action details are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The completed package is professionally usable by Karin Sheridan and cross-functional stakeholders without asking the author to resolve the central ambiguity: it supports an approval decision, makes responsibilities and safeguards concrete, and avoids procedural clutter unrelated to the decision.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Holistic review of PDF, XLSX, and PPTX","grader_hint":"Judge whether a real stakeholder could approve or challenge the selected course from the package and understand what happens next. Reward disciplined synthesis, clear terminology, and proportionate detail rather than decorative volume."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an internal investigative decision package for a fictional network-services organisation responding to a client-reported equipment diversion in England. The supervisor must turn sparse but complete stakeholder material into a defensible operating position without confusing intelligence with evidence or allowing a client promise to determine professional judgment.","difficulty_sources":["Bounded choice between rapid disruption and evidential caution when both readings remain defensible","Reconciliation of an informal client promise with fairness, privacy, disclosure, and professional authority boundaries","Transformation of sparse operational records and a second-hand interview account into a modeled, mutually consistent decision package","Need to actively prevent intelligence from being misused as proof while still supporting a timely operational decision"],"authoring_plan":{"source_file":{"filename":"everstead_case_notes.docx","format":"docx","role":"Create one compact factual case file containing stakeholder emails, messages, dispatch information, access observations, and an interview note. It is the sole supplied source and must not contain a pre-written recommendation or a legal rulebook.","facts_to_include":["An incident report dated 9 June 2025 records 18 equipment units across three dispatch lots between 22 May and 4 June 2025.","Eleven units have been reconciled to authorised destinations; seven remain unaccounted for. The replacement exposure recorded by the client is £31,800.","The last known diversion-related dispatch event was 4 June 2025. A collection-bay access permission remains active through a scheduled collection on 16 June 2025, creating a plausible continuing operational concern.","The dispatch ledger uses the same exception code across the three lots. Access records show three staff badge events overlapping relevant collection windows, but the records do not establish who handled or redirected the equipment.","An interview note records a second-hand account that Employee A said Employee B arranged redirection to an unapproved collection point. The note makes clear that the witness did not see the event and cannot independently verify the account.","The records do not establish a named perpetrator, a completed criminal offence by any particular person, or a basis for immediate identity disclosure.","A message from the client-facing stakeholder asks for suspected staff identities quickly, while an internal message records that this expectation has already been promised to the client.","The decision is required by Friday, 13 June 2025, and the factual position is current as of 12 June 2025."],"construction_constraints":["Use only the permitted people and organisations from the task context, plus neutral labels such as Employee A and Employee B.","Keep observations, quotations, and recorded stakeholder positions distinguishable from inferences.","Do not embed the answer, a preferred option, or specific legal resolution in the source."]},"professional_solution_specification":{"acceptable_recommendations":["A rapid targeted-interview recommendation is acceptable if the package makes the urgency proportionate, limits the scope, protects fairness and information handling, and does not treat the interview allegation as proof.","A staged intelligence-led investigation recommendation is acceptable if it is concrete, time-bounded, preserves the opportunity to obtain relevant information, and does not become an indefinite deferral that ignores the active collection-bay risk."],"required_synthesis":["Use the supplied facts as the factual baseline and preserve the 18, 11, 7, and £31,800 relationships.","Compare both options on speed, evidential value, fairness, disclosure risk, operational protection, and likely downstream rework or escalation.","State assumptions for any projected timing, probability, workload, or outcome. Do not invent extra incident figures.","Make one cross-file recommendation and authorize it with owner, timing, scope, and a review or stop condition.","Record the changed operating position, especially the qualification of the earlier client expectation about sharing identities.","Treat the second-hand interview account as intelligence or a lead unless independently corroborated. The selected plan must establish a clear barrier against identity disclosure, disciplinary action, or a guilt conclusion based solely on it.","Apply England-specific standards as at 12 June 2025 with appropriately bounded language. The solution should distinguish lawful investigative or employment steps from powers that a private organisation does not automatically possess.","Include fair interview and employment-process safeguards, preservation and provenance controls for records, and proportionate need-to-know handling of identities.","Make the decision usable for an internal stakeholder who was not present during the investigation."],"gold_derivation":"Build the reference solution with the same three deliverable types. Select either option only after comparing both; do not score the option choice itself as right or wrong. Derive the workbook's scenario outputs from labelled assumptions and connect the memorandum and deck to those outputs. Review all artifacts side by side for exact agreement on the selected option, fixed facts, assumptions, evidence-status language, and authorized action."},"review_method":"Render the PDF and PPTX and inspect the workbook formulas and displayed values offline. Apply the rubric item by item, accepting either recommendation when it meets the stated safeguards and proportionality conditions. Use professional judgment for legal-boundary accuracy and usability rather than requiring a particular layout or a fixed list of citations."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"everstead_case_notes","filename":"everstead_case_notes.docx","purpose":"The sole factual source for the decision package: stakeholder communications, incident facts, dispatch and access observations, interview material, timing, and the existing client promise.","required":true,"discovery":"Open this attached Word document before preparing any of the three deliverables; do not rely on invented facts outside it."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"decision_memorandum","filename":"everstead_decision_memorandum.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A concise decision memorandum that compares both defensible readings, makes one explicit recommendation, projects consequences, addresses the client promise and evidential safeguards, and authorizes the selected next action.","required":true,"constraints":["Maximum three pages.","Written for Karin Sheridan and cross-functional internal stakeholders.","Must identify assumptions and distinguish intelligence, allegation, and evidence.","Must end with the decision rather than an uncommitted list of possible next steps."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"options_assumptions_workbook","filename":"everstead_options_assumptions.xlsx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet","description":"An auditable workbook comparing the two options, recording factual inputs and assumptions, projecting outcomes and risks, and showing how the selected option supports the recommendation.","required":true,"constraints":["Use the case notes as the factual basis.","Separate fixed facts from assumptions, estimates, and scenario outputs.","Calculations must be traceable and agree with the memorandum and briefing deck.","Do not add unsupported case figures or identities."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"stakeholder_briefing","filename":"everstead_stakeholder_briefing.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"A short stakeholder briefing that communicates the situation, option comparison, chosen decision, projected implications, safeguards, and authorized action.","required":true,"constraints":["Maximum six slides.","Suitable for a cross-functional internal audience.","Use the same recommendation, figures, terminology, assumptions, and action ownership as the other deliverables.","Make the warning against treating an uncorroborated allegation as proof operationally clear."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use a domain-informed reviewer for investigative, fairness, information-handling, and England-jurisdiction boundaries, plus an independent reviewer for cross-file consistency, model auditability, page or slide limits, and practical stakeholder usability. Do not require a particular option when both are defensibly supported.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The case is fictional and the source provides operational facts rather than legal advice.","Legal review should assess accurate high-level boundaries and appropriate qualification as of the stated date, not demand a full legal opinion or one mandatory citation list.","Because two readings are intentionally defensible, the recommendation must be judged by reasoning, safeguards, proportionality, and execution readiness rather than by selecting a predetermined option."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:22:58+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:22:58+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:22:58+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:22:58+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/4304bbddc064698e","declared_deliverables":["everstead_decision_memorandum.pdf","everstead_options_assumptions.xlsx","everstead_stakeholder_briefing.pptx"]};
